  "translation": "These last few days, lots of friends have asked me: what's the difference between a qipao, hanfu and a Tang jacket? Today I'll explain, simply. Hanfu is the oldest, with two or three thousand years of history, much older than the qipao. Hanfu is usually long, with wide, full sleeves. It looks wonderful on, but it isn't as practical as a qipao. In the last few years more and more young people have started wearing hanfu to take photos in the park, and some even wear it out and about. The qipao became fashionable in Shanghai a hundred years ago. It's much shorter than hanfu, and much tighter, with a fairly high collar. Many women used to wear a qipao every day. Now it's mostly for weddings or special events. Both men and women can wear a Tang jacket. It has the character 唐 in its name, but it has very little to do with the Tang dynasty. Chinese people living abroad used to call themselves 'Tang people', which is where we get 'Tang people street', Chinatown, and also the 'Tang jacket'. What am I wearing to my graduation show opening? My mum's sent me a red qipao from Chengdu, so that's the one! My cousin's wearing his Tang jacket. And Mark? He says it's a secret.",
