  "translation": "Ài Mǎ: I spent the whole morning looking at oil paintings in the National Gallery. My eyes are tired. What about you? Mark: We've been looking at Chinese paintings. Xiaoyu, what exactly is the difference between Chinese painting and oil painting? Xiǎo Yǔ: First, the tools are different. Chinese painting uses a brush and ink, usually on paper. Oil painting uses oil paints, on canvas. Ài Mǎ: And the colours. Oil paintings have lots of strong colour, and a lot of Chinese paintings only use black. Xiǎo Yǔ: But there's more than one kind of black in Chinese painting. Ink can be dark or pale, and painters use dark and pale to show what's near and far. Mark: And the empty space. Oil painters usually cover the whole canvas, and Chinese painting likes to leave some parts unpainted. Ài Mǎ: Right. Lots of oil painters want to paint exactly what they see. Xiǎo Yǔ: Chinese painters are a bit different. Qi Baishi said the best painting is somewhere between 'like' and 'not like'. Ài Mǎ: Interesting! What about Xu Beihong? What he studied in Paris was oil painting. Xiǎo Yǔ: Yes. He brought Western ways of painting into Chinese painting, so his horses are both true to life and full of energy. Mark: East meets West! Just like Xiaoyu's graduation show: London painted the Chinese way. Ài Mǎ: So which of you can do Chinese painting? Xiǎo Yǔ: I can a bit. Mark, next time I'll teach you to paint shrimps! Mark: Great! But my shrimps will definitely be somewhere between 'like' and 'not like'... more like fish!",
