  "translation": "Mark: Happy New Year! Wishing you both a very happy New Year! Chen Ming's dad: Thank you, thank you, Happy New Year! Here, this red envelope is for you. Mark: No, no, I can't! I'm a grown man. How can I take a red envelope from you? Chen Ming's mum: If you're not married, you're still a child! Go on, take it. It's New Year money, for luck. Chen Ming: Mark, you can't refuse a red envelope from your elders. Take it with both hands and say thank you. That's all. Mark: Well... thank you! Wishing you both good health, and may everything go well for you! Chen Ming's mum: Oh, what a sweet talker! Come and have breakfast. There are still loads of dumplings. Mark: More dumplings? I ate over thirty last night and didn't get a single coin... Chen Ming's dad: Then keep eating today! Oh, by the way, could you follow the gala last night? Mark: Everything except the comic dialogues. They talk too fast. Chen Ming: Comic dialogues are full of Beijing slang. It's normal not to understand them. Mark: And at midnight I only heard a few firecrackers. I thought there'd be firecrackers going off everywhere in Beijing at New Year. Chen Ming's dad: It used to be like that. The hutongs were full of firecrackers. Now you can't set them off in town, for safety and for the air. If you want to, you have to go a long way out. Chen Ming's mum: It's better without them, safer and cleaner. Come on, come on, eat up. Dumplings are no good once they've gone cold!",
