  "translation": "Wáng + police officer (polite form of address): Take a seat. You're here to report a crime, I take it? What was stolen? Mark: My phone. At about half past six yesterday evening, on the Northern line. Wáng + police officer (polite form of address): What kind of phone? Mark: A black one. I've had it just over a year. It's got a blue case on it, and there's a bank card in the case too. Wáng + police officer (polite form of address): Have you reported the card lost? Mark: I did it last night, and I locked the phone online too. Wáng + police officer (polite form of address): Good. Did you notice anyone odd near you at the time? Mark: No. The carriage was really crowded and I was reading the whole time. I didn't notice a thing. Chen Ming: Officer, does this happen a lot? My bike got nicked last year too, and it's never turned up. Wáng + police officer (polite form of address): Quite a lot. Thieves usually strike when people are getting on and off. The more crowded it is, the easier it is to get robbed. Mark: So is there any chance of getting my phone back? Wáng + police officer (polite form of address): To be honest, not much. Fill in this form first, and if we hear anything we'll let you know straight away. ...Your Chinese is pretty good. How long have you been learning? Mark: Nearly two years. Next month I'm presenting our designs to a client in Shanghai, in Chinese. Wáng + police officer (polite form of address): Then good luck with it! You can always buy another phone, but the Chinese you've learned, nobody can steal that.",
