  "translation": "I've been learning Chinese for nearly two years. Three months ago, for the first time, I presented our design proposal to a client in Shanghai in Chinese. Lately friends keep asking me: 'What's your secret?' Actually, there's nothing magic about my secrets, and there are only four of them. First, learn a little every day. I'm busy at work, so I just listen to a podcast for twenty minutes on the Tube every day and learn ten new words. Twenty minutes isn't long, but over a year, it adds up to more than a hundred hours. Second, don't be afraid of getting it wrong. At first I made mistakes all the time. I even once said 'sleep' instead of 'dumplings'. My friends laughed, helped me put it right, and I've never forgotten it since. Third, find a good teacher and good friends. I met Xiaoyu at the language exchange. She's taught me for two years and has never once laughed at my pronunciation. I've eaten Mrs Lin's twice-cooked pork in Chengdu, and spent Spring Festival with Chen Ming's family in Beijing. Experiences like these have made me more and more interested in Chinese. Fourth, learn about what you love. I love food, so the first things I learned were the names of dishes. If you love music, listen to Chinese songs. If you love films, watch Chinese films. Now I'm learning how to write work emails in Chinese. Learning Chinese is like climbing the Great Wall: the higher you go, the more tiring it gets, but the view gets better and better too. Keep going!",
