  "translation": "Mark: Lily, your class was really good fun. Those kids' Chinese is more natural than mine! Huang Li (Lily): Most of them speak Chinese at home. It was different when I was little. My family spoke Cantonese, and I only learned Mandarin after I went to university. Mark: Really? No wonder your English name is Lily Wong, not Huang. Huang Li (Lily): Yes, in Cantonese 黄 is pronounced Wong. My family's been in Australia for over a hundred years. They say the first one to come here came for the gold. Mark: So that's how it is! Oh, by the way, Duoduo says her dad's colleagues all call him 老周, but her granny calls him 小周. So is he old or little? Huang Li (Lily): Ha ha, neither 老 nor 小 is about age here. His colleagues call him 老周 because they're close. His mum calls him 小周 because to her, he'll always be a child. Mark: 老 is a busy little word! Teacher, boss, tiger, mouse... and mice aren't old at all. Huang Li (Lily): That 老 doesn't really mean anything. It's just a prefix. There are suffixes too, like 家: painter, writer, scientist. Mark: 者 as well, right? Author, reader, journalist, and me two years ago: a beginner! Huang Li (Lily): You stopped being a beginner long ago. Here are two more: 化 and 性. 现代 plus 化 makes... Mark: 现代化, modernisation! So 国际化 means becoming more international? Huang Li (Lily): Exactly! Melbourne's a very international city. And 性: take 重要性 and 可能性. They're about how important something is, how likely it is. Mark: I get it! Once you've learned these, you can work out what new words mean. Huang Li (Lily): Exactly. Are you coming again next week? The kids all love their 'laowai teacher'!",
