  "translation": "This week I made an important decision: I'm not changing jobs. Here's what happened. Two weeks ago a big tech company contacted me through a headhunter, offering half as much again as I earn now. I'd be lying if I said I wasn't tempted. In Shanghai, rent, food, transport: what doesn't cost money? Several friends of mine have gone to big tech companies, and they're always telling me: 'Earn as much as you can while you're young.' But when I calmed down and thought about it, that job doesn't fit my field at all. What I love most is helping a brand find a look that's its own, not moving buttons around all day. Rather than doing a well-paid job I don't enjoy, I'd do better to get better at what I love. Of course, passion alone isn't enough: the rent still has to be paid. So I plucked up my courage and had a talk with my boss. To my surprise, she not only gave me a raise, but also put me in charge of Fuhe's new project next year. I get to keep doing what I love and I've got more opportunities: two birds with one stone. Some people say the young shouldn't flit from job to job, and should stay in any job for at least three years. I don't entirely agree. There's nothing wrong with changing jobs in itself. What matters is being clear about what you want. If you leave just because you're tired or fed up, you may well be giving up halfway. But if you know where you're going, go boldly. As for me? I'd rather go a bit more slowly and walk the road I've chosen. Finally, thanks to the British colleague in our office who leaves on the dot of six every day. He once said something I really like: 'We work in order to live, not live in order to work.'",
