  "translation": "Our reporter writes: With the New Year approaching, Zhuxi village in the hills of western Zhejiang is far busier than in previous years. According to the village's bamboo-weaving co-operative, in the month to 20 January it sold more than 8,000 bamboo baskets, fruit trays and other items by online livestream, with sales of over 600,000 yuan, five times the figure for the same period last year. Zhuxi is surrounded by bamboo forest, and villagers have woven baskets for several hundred years. As young people left to work elsewhere, only a dozen or so elderly weavers remained, and the craft was at one point in danger of dying out. The turning point came last year. In summer 2029 the village set up a bamboo-weaving co-operative to buy and sell the weavers' work together, and asked a Shanghai design agency to create a new brand and packaging for its products. In early December, villager Wu Chuan posted a short video online of his grandmother weaving a basket, and it quickly passed three million views. It is understood that Wu returned home from Hangzhou three years ago and sells bamboo ware by livestream every evening. 'A stream used to get a few dozen viewers; now it's often two or three thousand.' Wu said that on the busiest day he received more than a thousand orders. The surge in orders has brought new problems. 'The old weavers can make two or three a day at most, so many orders won't ship until after Spring Festival.' He Guoping, head of the co-operative, said it was organising young people in the village to learn to weave, and more than twenty had already signed up. Experts believe short video has brought new opportunities for rural crafts, but that traffic comes and goes quickly. 'Whether an internet hit can become a lasting brand still depends on quality.' It is understood that the co-operative plans to open bamboo-weaving classes for visitors this spring, and welcomes people from the cities to come and learn.",
