  "translation": "Nuonuo: it's late, and you may be asleep already. Some things I can't bring myself to say to your face, and on the phone we'd only end up arguing, so I want to write them down. Read this slowly when you have time. I know you were tired and angry over those three days at Qingming. Thinking about it now, Mum was in too much of a hurry. A new person every day: anyone would have had enough. The day you left, you didn't even look back at the station, and it hurt. You may think we're old-fashioned. But you should know how your dad and I got married. In 1996 I was an accountant at the factory, and an older colleague introduced me to your dad. We met three times, went to one film, and six months later we were married. In those days that's how everyone did it. Nobody asked whether you were in love, only whether you were suited. In all these years we've had plenty of rows, and been through plenty of hard times together, but looking back, having someone beside you gives you peace of mind. That's why we've always hoped you'd have a family of your own soon. Every time a relative asks about you and says, 'Nuonuo has so much going for her, how come she's still left on the shelf?', I smile, then go home and can't sleep. The truth is I hate the phrase 'leftover woman' too. My daughter has made her own way in Shanghai and stood on her own two feet. How could she be 'left over'? When it comes down to it, what Mum fears isn't that you won't marry. It's that you'll have too hard a time on your own: nobody to bring you a glass of water when you're ill, nobody to talk to when you're sad. When your dad and I are old, and gone, who'll keep you company? But these last few days I've come to see something clearly: marriage isn't a task, and you can't just pick anyone for the sake of getting it done. Times have changed, after all. Your generation has its own ideas and its own choices. Rather than push you into meeting people you don't like, better to let you live the life you want. From now on, I won't arrange any more dates for you. If the relatives ask again, I'll say: my daughter is doing very well. If you meet someone you like, bring them home for us to see; if you don't, that's all right too. The door here is always open for you. Get an early night, and don't work late all the time. Mum",
