  "translation": "Qian Meihua is sixty-nine. She was born in an old house in a lane off Yuyuan Road, and has lived there all her life. The room I rent now happens to be the very room she was born in. As she remembers it, in the sixties this little three-storey house held five households, more than twenty people, and even the space under the stairs was home to a married couple. Every morning each household lit its coal stove at the door, and the whole lane filled with smoke. 'Back then you needed coupons for everything,' she says. 'Braised pork once a month was like New Year.' Yet in her memory, those were also the liveliest days. On summer evenings the neighbours carried bamboo chairs out into the lane to sit in the cool, and whoever had cooked something good would always take a bowl next door. In her words: 'We were poor then, but every door was open.' In 1979, at eighteen, Qian Meihua went to work in a textile mill in Yangpu, on rotating shifts, and stayed eighteen years. Meanwhile the city around her was changing enormously: reform and opening up, the development of Pudong, tower after tower going up. In 1997 the mill stopped production and she was laid off. She was thirty-six, her son was nine, and her husband was working in another city. She rarely talks about those years. It was her son who told us how his mother looked for work all day, knitted under the lamp at night, and took the jumpers out to sell by the road the next morning. At this, Auntie Qian waved her hand: 'That's all in the past. Lots of us women were laid off then. We all got through it the same way.' Today her son lives on the twentieth-something floor of a tower in Pudong, and her grandson, who is ten, has four cram classes at the weekend. As she sees it, her generation 'went without everything, except time', and her son's 'has everything, except time'. As for her grandson's generation, she laughs: 'I can't make them out, and I don't need to.' By the time the interview ended, it was dark. Auntie Qian got up, switched on the light, washed the teacups on the table one by one and put them back where they belonged. I couldn't help thinking that this kitchen once held five coal stoves and the voices of more than twenty people, and now there is one person and one lamp. Looking back, in more than a year in Shanghai I've written about the city's plane trees, its lanes and its breakfasts, but I had never seriously asked how the people who live here got to where they are. Perhaps the most precious thing a city has isn't its new towers, but people like Auntie Qian, and everything they remember.",
