  "translation": "Teachers, dear friends: good afternoon! First of all, thank you, Lucy, for inviting me. One Saturday afternoon four years ago, I stood outside that door, not daring to come in. A young woman came over and asked me: 'Excuse me, are you Mark?' I was so nervous I could barely tell her my own surname. That day I learned my first words of Chinese: nǐ hǎo. Today I'd like to tell you, in Chinese, about these four years. People often ask me what's hardest about learning Chinese. My answer is: not the tones, not the characters, but keeping going. At the start I said 'sleep' for 'dumplings' and typed 'please search' for 'please find attached', made a fool of myself plenty of times, and thought about giving up. But there's an old Chinese saying: 'A journey of a thousand miles begins with a single step.' I had no secret. I just listened to twenty minutes of podcast on the Tube each day, wrote three sentences of diary each day, and kept going, one step at a time. Chinese has a word, 步步, 'step by step'. There are no shortcuts in learning a language: you can only go forward, step by step. So what has Chinese given me? If in the first year it gave me a few friends, then later it gave me several homes: in Chengdu, in Beijing, in Melbourne, in a lane in Shanghai. I've eaten the spiciest hotpot at the Lins', spent New Year's Eve on the Chens' sofa, and in a Shanghai lady's kitchen heard the story of her sixty-nine years. Chinese has taught me that 'have you eaten?' can sometimes be warmer than 'hello', and that 'take your time' may be the best encouragement in the world. More than that, Chinese has given me a new pair of eyes to see the world with. I used to think the world looked like London. Later I understood that people look at the same moon differently in Chengdu, in Melbourne and in Shanghai. Every language you learn gives you another life. Today there's a new friend at the door. He's been learning Chinese for just two weeks, and a moment ago he was standing outside, exactly as I was four years ago. Tom, I want to say to you: don't be afraid of getting it wrong, because that's how you remember; don't do it by halves, and don't give up halfway. One day you'll find that every step you've taken counts. Finally, I'd like to thank Xiaoyu, my first Chinese teacher and my best friend; Chen Ming, who has fed me dumplings for four years and laughed at my lateness for just as long; David, Emma and Yumi; and this café. Confucius said: 'Is it not a joy to have friends come from afar?' Today my friends have come here from Tokyo, from Shanghai and from every corner of London, and I really am very happy. There's no end to learning, and I still have a lot of Chinese to learn. But I'm not afraid any more. Thank you, everyone!",
